From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kai Ninomiya <kainino@chromium.org>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 14:20:40 -0700
Subject: [metal] Handle command buffer execution errors

Originally authored by Gregg but with some major iterations.

- Dawn previously ignored if a Metal command buffer failed to complete.
  This would result in Dawn continuing onward as if it had succeeded.

  Fix this by moving the device to the Disconnecting state immediately
  inside the completion handler, and updating Disconnect() to handle the
  fact that the state could have been changed silently from another
  thread.

- Make MapAsyncEvent::Complete() check for device loss just before
  completing the map. This prevents exposing data that could be stale
  due to a prior command buffer having failed to actually execute, which
  was at least necessary when we inject a fake command buffer failure. I
  am not sure if this has any real impact on this or other backends, but
  it seems more correct anyway.

Fixed: 553115993
Co-authored-by: Gregg Tavares <gman@chromium.org>
Change-Id: I16ae7672ede307ee6f7565cdb5274ae9356dc556
Reviewed-on: https://dawn-review.googlesource.com/c/dawn/+/340135
Commit-Queue: Kai Ninomiya <kainino@chromium.org>
Reviewed-by: Corentin Wallez <cwallez@chromium.org>
Reviewed-by: Loko Kung <lokokung@google.com>

Electron adaptation notes (44-x-y):
Dawn on this branch predates the Disconnecting/Disconnect() split: the
device state is named BeingDisconnected and the GPU-timeline drain still
lives inline in DeviceBase::Destroy(). Accordingly SetDisconnectingIfAlive()
is added (declared in Device.h, which upstream already had) using
State::BeingDisconnected, the Metal completion handler's state check uses
BeingDisconnected, and the two IgnoreErrors() waits in Destroy() become
std::ignore = ConsumedError(...) exactly as upstream changed them in
Disconnect() (Destroy() emits the DeviceLost callback before this point on
both versions). Buffer.h gains only the UnmapEarly() declaration (the
GetMappedRangeInternal() context line does not exist here), the completed
handler keeps the branch's TRACE_EVENT_ASYNC_END0 macro, and
webgpu-cts/expectations.txt keeps the branch's content.

